{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785092247,"nonce":"1vBn1v9cEPlpy9nX2ZCSSNjit2U=","title":"ADR-0061 s3-drain: replace the drain tunnel and the artifact-harvest tunnel","message":"s3-feed replaces the FEED leg (the workspace service + a Scarab-owned\nfetcher image). This slice does the other direction, and it must cover\n**three** `exec` tars, not one.\n\nThe naming matters because \"s2\" and \"s3\" both meant \"delete the tunnel\" to\ntwo different agents. Settled vocabulary:\n\n- **s3-feed** — replace the feed tunnel with the Scarab-owned fetcher\n  (`docker/wsfetch/`). Ships first, needs no privileges.\n- **s3-drain** — this ticket.\n- **s2b** — the CSI/FUSE node driver.\n\nDepends on: s1, s3-feed. Best done after s2b so the drain can use the\noverlay upper layer (s6 / 66fc0e3), but not blocked on it.\n\n---\n\n\nVerified at `bd30a71`:\n\n1. **Feed** — `crates/scarab-executor-k8s/src/lib.rs:461` `exec_with_stdin`\n   with `tar -xf - -C /workspace \u0026\u0026 chmod -R g+rwX … \u0026\u0026 touch\n   /scarab-ctl/init-done`. **Owned by s3-feed.**\n2. **Drain** — `:505` `exec_capture_stdout` with\n   `tar -cf - -C /workspace .`, then server-side `unpack_dir` +\n   `Cas::ingest`.\n3. **Artifact harvest** — `:673`, inside `harvest_artifacts` (`:649`):\n   `tar -cf - -C /scarab/artifacts . 2\u003e/dev/null || true`, then\n   `unpack_dir` + per-file upload to `artifacts/{run}/{name}` (ADR-0052).\n   **ADR-0061 does not mention this one at all.** It runs for *every*\n   terminated step at *any* exit code, so it is on every boundary in the\n   budget. The s0 instrumentation comment at `:671` already says so.\n\nBoth (2) and (3) must be replaced in this slice, or the tunnel is only\ntwo-thirds deleted and the ADR's part 3 claim (\"the `kubectl exec` tar\ntunnel is deleted\") is false.\n\nReplacement shape: the egress container becomes a Scarab-owned image that\n`POST`s blobs and trees straight to the workspace service\n(`PUT /v1/cas/blobs/{hash}`, `PUT /v1/cas/trees/{hash}`,\n`POST /v1/cas/have` to skip what the service already holds), authenticated\nwith the s1 workspace token, and reports **one root hash** back. Artifacts\ntake the same route with their own destination. The server then exchanges\ntens of bytes, per ADR-0061 part 3. `WORKSPACE_HELPER_IMAGE`\n(`busybox:1.36`) retires with the doorstops.\n\n\nThis is not only a performance slice. Filed separately as a bug for the case\nwhere s3-drain does not land live-verified, but it is the reason this slice\nis a correctness fix and not a refactor:\n\n`exec_capture_stdout` (`lib.rs:801`) sets `.stderr(false)` (`:812`) and never\ncalls `take_status()`. In kube-4, `AttachedProcess::join()` (`:824`) returns\n`Err` only for websocket/task failures — the remote command's exit `Status`\nframe goes to a separate `status_rx`\n(`kube-client-4.0.0/src/api/remote_command.rs:347-352`). So a\n`tar -cf - -C /workspace .` that dies partway yields **truncated stdout that\nis not an error**. `unpack_dir` only fails if the truncation lands mid-header;\ntruncation at a record boundary unpacks cleanly into a *partial tree*, which\nis then `ingest`ed and published as the step's authoritative\n`scarab.io/workspace-root`.\n\nThat is the one place where \"unresumable\" upgrades from *slow* to **wrong**.\nCorroboration: `crates/scarab-executor-k8s/tests/cluster.rs:37-48` tolerates\ntransient `workspace: broken pipe` at roughly 1-in-9 on CI kind\n(git-bug a0b42ad).\n\nThe replacement must be structurally incapable of this: the helper knows the\nfile list, computes each hash, and the service rejects a hash mismatch at\nthe door (400). A partial push cannot produce a valid root.\n\n\n- `harvest_artifacts` currently swallows failure by design\n  (`2\u003e/dev/null || true`) because an empty artifacts dir tars to nothing.\n  The replacement needs an explicit \"no artifacts\" signal instead of a\n  swallowed error, or the same silent-partial class reappears for artifacts.\n- Keep `crates/scarab-storage-s3/tests/fidelity.rs` passing — s7's\n  mode/mtime/symlink contract is inherited by whatever replaces the tar\n  legs. ADR-0061's Open section says so explicitly.\n- `ANNOTATION_WS_INPUTS` is inserted even when empty, so \"is this a\n  workspace Pod\" = annotation *present* while \"does it need a feed\" =\n  annotation *non-empty*. Two predicates on one field; untangle while in\n  here (see also s8, 4f15a66).\n\n\n- That the replacement is **faster** than the `exec` tar tunnel. s0's\n  before-numbers are committed in ADR-0061's Open section; the drain leg was\n  385 ms `exec` / 939 ms server tar / 8867 ms CAS at 2000 files. Note the\n  tunnel is only 4–15% of the boundary — most of the win here is correctness\n  and removing the API-server abuse, not wall-clock.\n- That mode/mtime survive the round trip **through the service**, as\n  distinct from through the CAS (s7 proves the latter only).","files":null}]}